+## Review repair 2026-10-25 — distinct-column shortcut
+
+**Observed defect.** In the version-3.0.0 easy lesson at seed 0, demonstrations
+3–6 gave the globally winning color exactly one occupied cell per occupied
+column. The executable alternative “choose the color occupying the most
+columns, and use that number as the bar length” therefore fit all four visible
+demonstrations. It disagreed on query index 7, where the winner reused a
+column. The exact pre-repair lesson and source are retained under
+`counterexamples/review-b479175913430c66-v0001/`.
+
+**Repair.** Version 3.0.1 constructs the compact-runner winner with two
+same-column cells chosen from the checkerboard isolation pool. They remain
+separate 4-connected singleton components, preserving the existing
+largest-component witness, while the winner's distinct-column count is now
+strictly below its global count. The new `most_distinct_columns` semantic rival
+and witness are part of `LESSON_COMPETITORS`, so the ordinary four-control
+curriculum mechanically rejects the shortcut.
+
+**Verification.** The focused task suite passes 10 tests. The source-bound tool
+`workspace/most_columns_audit.py` replayed the retained defect and then checked
+48 repaired lessons (240 generated pairs) across all three bands, seeds 0 and
+17, and eight consecutive starts. Protected Racket executed 490 labels/rival
+outputs; all authority labels matched, every lesson rejected the shortcut from
+demonstrations alone, and every lesson gate passed. This is a finite audit of
+one declared alternative, not a uniqueness proof over arbitrary programs.
+
+Final generator SHA-256 for this repair:
+`17bb189d0565331866e2e0600b4de6d6786f13a98a206ee285c1337e14b6a15c`.
